      "tense": "subjonctif-present",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "In short",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "en": "Some conjunctions always take the subjunctive. Others never do. Learn the two lists side by side."
            },
            {
              "type": "table",
              "head": [
                "+ subjunctive",
                "meaning",
                "+ indicative",
                "meaning"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "pour que",
                  "so that",
                  "parce que",
                  "because"
                ],
                [
                  "avant que",
                  "before",
                  "après que",
                  "after"
                ],
                [
                  "bien que",
                  "although",
                  "pendant que",
                  "while"
                ],
                [
                  "sans que",
                  "without",
                  "quand",
                  "when"
                ],
                [
                  "à condition que",
                  "provided that",
                  "si",
                  "if"
                ],
                [
                  "jusqu'à ce que",
                  "until",
                  "depuis que",
                  "since"
                ]
              ],
              "textCols": [
                1,
                3
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Same subject: preposition + infinitive",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "en": "When both parts have the same subject, use the preposition with the infinitive instead: *pour partir, avant de partir, sans dire*. *Je travaille pour réussir* (I work to succeed) but *Je travaille pour que mes parents soient contents*."
            },
            {
              "type": "check",
